    "title": "CMPD, community leaders spread 'cool' cheer on hot day - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Brien Blakely ,\n1 hours ago\nCHARLOTTE, N.C. (QUEEN CITY NEWS) — Charlotte-Mecklenburg Police officers joined forces with community activists Wednesday to spread some cool cheer, with a message of keeping kids on the straight and narrow.\nPromise Youth Development’s Tesha Boyd says positive role models and support start in the home, but are needed all around the east Charlotte neighborhood she serves.\nBoyd talks with QCN Anchor Brien Blakely. “Influence comes from a lot of different ways, but I tell ya, they have access to social media and different things, so the teen takeovers are not just local to Charlotte,” Boyd said. “It’s happening all over. They copy and paste what they see happening, so positive influences can make a significant influence on a child’s life, and that’s what we’re trying to do.”\nBoyd helped provide pizza and ice cream as temperatures soared into the upper-90s.\nQueen City News show “On the Record” will dive into the teen takeovers Sunday at 8:30 am.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to Queen City News.",
    "summary": "CHARLOTTE, N.C. (QUEEN CITY NEWS) — Charlotte-Mecklenburg Police officers joined forces with community activists Wednesday to spread some cool cheer, with a message of keeping kids on the straight and narrow. Promise Youth Development’s Tesha Boyd says positive role models and support start in the home, but are needed all around the east Charlotte neighborhood she serves. “Influence comes from a lot of different ways, but I tell ya, they have access to social media and different things, so the teen takeovers are not just local to Charlotte,” Boyd said.",
